{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PMFeT9T124kGivU5vtZEnD","type":"tool_result","content":"2090\t### 10.6 Amendments after slice 6b (revision 4, 2026-10-13)\n2091\t\n2092\tEach item names the clause, what changed, and why; the clause itself\n2093\tcarries the same marker. Sources are the engineer report's design bugs\n2094\t(B1-B4) and deviations (1-10) and the three codex reviews' rulings.\n2095\t\n2096\t| # | Clause | What changed | Why |\n2097\t|---|---|---|---|\n2098\t| B1 | §5.2 rows 10-12, the install paragraph; §8.2 item 3 | row 10 is child `:blocked`, phase `:parked`; row 11 is the `:running` wire phase set by hand on row 10's machine; the lower's install contract (phase set, response and image, child `:blocked`) is stated | `advance-install` retains a blocked child at `:parked` and links a halted or parked one in the same sweep; the promised states were unreachable |\n2099\t| B2 | §5.2 wrapper paragraph, row 21 | the mechanical wrapper rule has one stated exception: row 21 under `:depth-3` answers `[3 [2 [1 1]]]` | `nest`'s parameter is `x` and shadows the free `x` before `f` is created; the reference run itself answers 1 |\n2100\t| B3 | §5.2 refusal rows; §8.2 item 7 | the `:tail` fourth slot is `:row-shape` at structure step 2, and the tamper is a raw row edit with rehash | the row table fixes `:tail` at three slots before S1 runs; `emit` drops extra abstract slots silently |\n2101\t| B4 | §7.1 | the lift's order is quiescence, kind, free environment, walk, code, dependency closure, trees, task, frames, emit | segments and contexts are known only after the walk |\n2102\t| D1 | §6.1, §8.2 depends-on | `:source-index` is a lift input from the composition | the kernel holds no source index |\n2103\t| D2 | §7.1 | refusals are thrown `ex-info` | consistency with `decode`/`emit` |\n2104\t| D3 | §2.5, §2.6, §7.2 | custody nil at the root; non-nil is `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` at step 10 | exclusive/holder is phase 7 |\n2105\t| D4 | §2.5, §5.2 row 17 | linked registry entries do not travel; the receiver's registry is a composition prerequisite and is kept as given | no registry slot in the row table |\n2106\t| D5 | §7.1, §7.2 | discharge is per context over live code (`live-pcs`), answered by `E`, the one active store, primitive, installing export, registry; lift only | codex F3 (union over stores was unsound) and F6 (whole-body scan refused valid states) |\n2107\t| D6 | §2.5 | the store slice is the whole root store; every parked record travels; module stores by reachability | conservative and what the code does |\n2108\t| D7 | §6.3 | the retention seam landed; the lift reads the kernel's `free-env` field by default, the option overrides, E2 catches a disagreeing override | 4b stage 3 landed before 6b |\n2109\t| D8 | §7.2 | the lower's four stages, each across the whole tree before the next, all before attachment; the refusal-kind table including `:yin.k/stores` | codex F1, F2, F4, F7, F8: every carrier defect must be a named refusal before any attach |\n2110\t| D9 | §5.2 row 18 | the fixture's module store is symbol-keyed and hand-built | codex asked for the synthetic label |\n2111\t| D10 | §9 | 4b stage 2 landed; rows 3-4, 7-12, 17, 24 run; three-host gate still open | honesty |\n2112\t\n2113\t**Note on `handoff.cljc` (no edit).** The engineer report said\n2114\t`export-task` builds installing names as `(symbol (str m \"/\" x))`, a\n2115\tnil-namespace symbol that never equals `m/x`. Read at\n2116\t`handoff.cljc:1480-1488`, the code is `(symbol (str (name m) \"/\" (name\n2117\tx)))`. The one-argument `symbol` of Clojure, ClojureScript and the\n2118\tpinned ClojureDart core splits its string at `/` into namespace and\n2119\tname, so this yields the qualified `host.mod/f` with namespace\n2120\t`\"host.mod\"`, equal to `'host.mod/f`; codex round 1 evaluated it on the\n2121\tJVM and found the same. The claim is withdrawn; the old form is not a\n2122\tbug. `rows.cljc`'s `installing-names` uses the two-argument `(symbol\n2123\t(name mn) (name x))`, which is equivalent. `handoff.cljc` is not edited\n2124\tby this phase (§8.5).\n2125\t\n2126\t---\n2127\t\n2128\t## 11. Owner questions\n2129\t\n2130\tRevisions 2-3 left none: the reviewer accepted the engine-less profile\n2131\tand rejected the merged-`E` free environment. **Revision 4 (after 6b)\n2132\tlists the design choices the merged code made that no ruling covers.**\n2133\tEach is stated as the code behaves today; none is decided by this\n2134\tdocument.\n2135\t\n2136\t- **Q1, install children, the parked-child scenario.** The engine never\n2137\t  retains an install child at a quiescent safepoint except `:blocked` at\n2138\t  phase `:parked` (§5.2 B1). The carrier still admits phase `:running`\n2139\t  (never engine-reachable at a safepoint) and the lower refuses any\n2140\t  child kind but `:blocked`. Should the row table and both checks\n2141\t  narrow to the one reachable state (phase `:parked`, child `:blocked`),\n2142\t  or does the owner want a reachable parked-child install scenario,\n2143\t  which is an engine change (`advance-install`), with row 11 rewritten\n2144\t  to it?\n2145\t- **Q2, census agreement on lower.** The draft §8 step 9 requires the\n2146\t  carried `requires`/`code` to agree **exactly** with a census recomputed\n2147\t  from the rows (a declared segment not reached, or a reached segment\n2148\t  not declared, is `:yin.k/undecodable`). `check-dependencies!` checks\n2149\t  only that `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code`; a vector\n2150\t  carried but referenced by no row is accepted and attached. Accept the\n2151\t  narrower check, or require the recomputed census?\n2152\t- **Q3, install response verification.** The draft §5.4 asks the\n2153\t  receiver to verify that the install's link id equals `parent`, that\n2154\t  the image hashes to the address it is delivered under and is the image\n2155\t  the manifest names, and that format, contract and the child's profile\n2156\t  agree. `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, export shape\n2157\t  and that the image loads; not the link id, not the image's address\n2158\t  against the manifest. Accept the narrower contract for phase 6, or\n2159\t  extend it before phase 7 (which re-bases the export-recovery object)?\n2160\t- **Q4, operation ids and correlation on lower.** The draft §8 step 8's\n2161\t  op-id rules (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent\n2162\t  without origin) and its correlation agreement (cursor-cell stream,\n2163\t  envelope ids, module names) are not implemented; op-ids travel\n2164\t  verbatim, and pending compatibility is only P2's reason check. With\n2165\t  fork-only custody there is no `next-op-seq`; is this phase 7's with\n2166\t  the custody arm, or a 6-era gap to close?\n2167\t- **Q5, no re-discharge on lower.** Free names are discharged at lift\n2168\t  only (§7.1); the lower checks carried segments, trees, store census,\n2169\t  closures and primitives, and a name a closure resolves through the\n2170\t  registry is a composition prerequisite (§2.5). A receiver lacking that\n2171\t  registry entry accepts the carrier and fails at run time\n2172\t  (`Unable to resolve symbol`). Accept, or require the lower to\n2173\t  re-discharge registry-answered names against the receiver's registry\n2174\t  before attachment?\n2175\t- **Q6, conservatism of the live-code rule.** `live-pcs` follows both\n2176\t  successor edges of every branch and enters every reachable closure\n2177\t  body, with no constant propagation. The harness's `(if false (fn []\n2178\t  ghost) (next-of c))` case transports only because the lift happens at\n2179\t  a safepoint **after** the branch (the resume pc is past it, so the\n2180\t  dead arm is unreachable by edges); the same program lifted at a\n2181\t  safepoint **before** the branch would refuse `ghost` although the arm\n2182\t  can never run. Is \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\"\n2183\t  the intended precision, accepting the false refusals it implies for\n2184\t  constant-dead code?\n2185\t- **Q7, host-class primitives behind a shadow.** `:host-state-primitive`\n2186\t  refuses only when the name reaches the primitive step of the answering\n2187\t  order; the same name bound in `E` or the active store lifts (codex\n2188\t  round 2 confirmed). Intended, or should a host-class primitive name\n2189\t  refuse regardless of shadowing?\n2190\t- **Q8, preflight attachment cost.** `attaches?` validates and analyses\n2191\t  every carried vector (and every install image) by attaching it into a\n2192\t  throwaway receiver, and restore attaches it again. Codex found it pure\n2193\t  and only duplicated CPU. Accept, or ask the kernel for a validate-only\n2194\t  entry point?\n2195\t- **Q9, Law 2 outside a fresh receiver.** The id counter is adopted as\n2196\t  `max(local, carried)`; a receiver whose construction mints more ids\n2197\t  than the carrier relifts to a different root (draft §9 already scopes\n2198\t  the law to the fresh-receiver context). No harness case has a\n2199\t  non-fresh receiver. Add one as a documented non-law, or leave it?\n2200\t- **Q10, the `:running` wire phase's future.** If Q1 narrows the phase\n2201\t  set, row 11 disappears from the matrix and the `:install` row's phase\n2202\t  slot becomes a constant; if not, row 11 stays a synthetic case. Which?\n2203\t\n2204\t---\n2205\t\n2206\t## 12. Portability notes for the implementer\n2207\t\n2208\t1. **Nil-valued window keys (Dart, Node).** The window is built with\n2209\t   `select-keys` and encoded as `[r [:lit nil]]`; never filter `some?`\n2210\t   values; `frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member` over row 22\n2211\t   (`(pair nil (next-of c))`, the nil operand evaluated before the read)\n2212\t   is the canary. Membership is always by `contains?`/key sets, never\n2213\t   `(get W r)`.\n2214\t2. **`for` over large seqs (Dart).** Row sets of the C4 modules run to\n2215\t   thousands of rows; build with `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`.\n2216\t3. **Reader whitespace before closers (Dart).** Pinned row sets are\n2217\t   Clojure data in `.cljc`, never EDN strings.\n2218\t4. **Duplicate protocol params (Dart).** `gc-children [_ _x]`\n2219\t   (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:386`); any new protocol method\n2220\t   in `layout` likewise.\n2221\t5. **Multi-key `assoc` on nil (Dart).** Frames and windows start from\n2222\t   `{}` (`handoff.cljc:1455-1457` records the trap).\n2223\t6. **Reader conditionals.** `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`\n2224\t   with `:cljd` first; `:clj`-only source-reading tests as `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`.\n2225\t7. **Private var access (Dart).** Every function named in §8 is public;\n2226\t   no `#'`.\n2227\t8. **Sorted keys.** Window and bindings rows are sorted by Jing\n2228\t   canonical bytes of the key, never by host `sort` on mixed types.\n2229\t9. **Numeric classes (Node).** `pc`, `rd`, `r`, `site` are exact\n2230\t   integers; compare with `cbor/numeric-kind` before `=` on wire values\n2231\t   (`ucf/profile-engine`, `ucf.cljc:77-91`, is the template).\n2232\t10. **Fresh worktree.** `mise trust` and `npm ci` before the Node lane."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47508f4a-f118-4fad-b5f9-49922c52f330","uuid":"469cf610-8f5c-49ea-a8b0-b6f84fee72d6","timestamp":"2026-10-13T08:29:54.362Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-docs-6c/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"### 10.6 Amendments after slice 6b (revision 4, 2026-10-13)\n\nEach item names the clause, what changed, and why; the clause itself\ncarries the same marker. Sources are the engineer report's design bugs\n(B1-B4) and deviations (1-10) and the three codex reviews' rulings.\n\n| # | Clause | What changed | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| B1 | §5.2 rows 10-12, the install paragraph; §8.2 item 3 | row 10 is child `:blocked`, phase `:parked`; row 11 is the `:running` wire phase set by hand on row 10's machine; the lower's install contract (phase set, response and image, child `:blocked`) is stated | `advance-install` retains a blocked child at `:parked` and links a halted or parked one in the same sweep; the promised states were unreachable |\n| B2 | §5.2 wrapper paragraph, row 21 | the mechanical wrapper rule has one stated exception: row 21 under `:depth-3` answers `[3 [2 [1 1]]]` | `nest`'s parameter is `x` and shadows the free `x` before `f` is created; the reference run itself answers 1 |\n| B3 | §5.2 refusal rows; §8.2 item 7 | the `:tail` fourth slot is `:row-shape` at structure step 2, and the tamper is a raw row edit with rehash | the row table fixes `:tail` at three slots before S1 runs; `emit` drops extra abstract slots silently |\n| B4 | §7.1 | the lift's order is quiescence, kind, free environment, walk, code, dependency closure, trees, task, frames, emit | segments and contexts are known only after the walk |\n| D1 | §6.1, §8.2 depends-on | `:source-index` is a lift input from the composition | the kernel holds no source index |\n| D2 | §7.1 | refusals are thrown `ex-info` | consistency with `decode`/`emit` |\n| D3 | §2.5, §2.6, §7.2 | custody nil at the root; non-nil is `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` at step 10 | exclusive/holder is phase 7 |\n| D4 | §2.5, §5.2 row 17 | linked registry entries do not travel; the receiver's registry is a composition prerequisite and is kept as given | no registry slot in the row table |\n| D5 | §7.1, §7.2 | discharge is per context over live code (`live-pcs`), answered by `E`, the one active store, primitive, installing export, registry; lift only | codex F3 (union over stores was unsound) and F6 (whole-body scan refused valid states) |\n| D6 | §2.5 | the store slice is the whole root store; every parked record travels; module stores by reachability | conservative and what the code does |\n| D7 | §6.3 | the retention seam landed; the lift reads the kernel's `free-env` field by default, the option overrides, E2 catches a disagreeing override | 4b stage 3 landed before 6b |\n| D8 | §7.2 | the lower's four stages, each across the whole tree before the next, all before attachment; the refusal-kind table including `:yin.k/stores` | codex F1, F2, F4, F7, F8: every carrier defect must be a named refusal before any attach |\n| D9 | §5.2 row 18 | the fixture's module store is symbol-keyed and hand-built | codex asked for the synthetic label |\n| D10 | §9 | 4b stage 2 landed; rows 3-4, 7-12, 17, 24 run; three-host gate still open | honesty |\n\n**Note on `handoff.cljc` (no edit).** The engineer report said\n`export-task` builds installing names as `(symbol (str m \"/\" x))`, a\nnil-namespace symbol that never equals `m/x`. Read at\n`handoff.cljc:1480-1488`, the code is `(symbol (str (name m) \"/\" (name\nx)))`. The one-argument `symbol` of Clojure, ClojureScript and the\npinned ClojureDart core splits its string at `/` into namespace and\nname, so this yields the qualified `host.mod/f` with namespace\n`\"host.mod\"`, equal to `'host.mod/f`; codex round 1 evaluated it on the\nJVM and found the same. The claim is withdrawn; the old form is not a\nbug. `rows.cljc`'s `installing-names` uses the two-argument `(symbol\n(name mn) (name x))`, which is equivalent. `handoff.cljc` is not edited\nby this phase (§8.5).\n\n---\n\n## 11. Owner questions\n\nRevisions 2-3 left none: the reviewer accepted the engine-less profile\nand rejected the merged-`E` free environment. **Revision 4 (after 6b)\nlists the design choices the merged code made that no ruling covers.**\nEach is stated as the code behaves today; none is decided by this\ndocument.\n\n- **Q1, install children, the parked-child scenario.** The engine never\n  retains an install child at a quiescent safepoint except `:blocked` at\n  phase `:parked` (§5.2 B1). The carrier still admits phase `:running`\n  (never engine-reachable at a safepoint) and the lower refuses any\n  child kind but `:blocked`. Should the row table and both checks\n  narrow to the one reachable state (phase `:parked`, child `:blocked`),\n  or does the owner want a reachable parked-child install scenario,\n  which is an engine change (`advance-install`), with row 11 rewritten\n  to it?\n- **Q2, census agreement on lower.** The draft §8 step 9 requires the\n  carried `requires`/`code` to agree **exactly** with a census recomputed\n  from the rows (a declared segment not reached, or a reached segment\n  not declared, is `:yin.k/undecodable`). `check-dependencies!` checks\n  only that `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code`; a vector\n  carried but referenced by no row is accepted and attached. Accept the\n  narrower check, or require the recomputed census?\n- **Q3, install response verification.** The draft §5.4 asks the\n  receiver to verify that the install's link id equals `parent`, that\n  the image hashes to the address it is delivered under and is the image\n  the manifest names, and that format, contract and the child's profile\n  agree. `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, export shape\n  and that the image loads; not the link id, not the image's address\n  against the manifest. Accept the narrower contract for phase 6, or\n  extend it before phase 7 (which re-bases the export-recovery object)?\n- **Q4, operation ids and correlation on lower.** The draft §8 step 8's\n  op-id rules (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent\n  without origin) and its correlation agreement (cursor-cell stream,\n  envelope ids, module names) are not implemented; op-ids travel\n  verbatim, and pending compatibility is only P2's reason check. With\n  fork-only custody there is no `next-op-seq`; is this phase 7's with\n  the custody arm, or a 6-era gap to close?\n- **Q5, no re-discharge on lower.** Free names are discharged at lift\n  only (§7.1); the lower checks carried segments, trees, store census,\n  closures and primitives, and a name a closure resolves through the\n  registry is a composition prerequisite (§2.5). A receiver lacking that\n  registry entry accepts the carrier and fails at run time\n  (`Unable to resolve symbol`). Accept, or require the lower to\n  re-discharge registry-answered names against the receiver's registry\n  before attachment?\n- **Q6, conservatism of the live-code rule.** `live-pcs` follows both\n  successor edges of every branch and enters every reachable closure\n  body, with no constant propagation. The harness's `(if false (fn []\n  ghost) (next-of c))` case transports only because the lift happens at\n  a safepoint **after** the branch (the resume pc is past it, so the\n  dead arm is unreachable by edges); the same program lifted at a\n  safepoint **before** the branch would refuse `ghost` although the arm\n  can never run. Is \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\"\n  the intended precision, accepting the false refusals it implies for\n  constant-dead code?\n- **Q7, host-class primitives behind a shadow.** `:host-state-primitive`\n  refuses only when the name reaches the primitive step of the answering\n  order; the same name bound in `E` or the active store lifts (codex\n  round 2 confirmed). Intended, or should a host-class primitive name\n  refuse regardless of shadowing?\n- **Q8, preflight attachment cost.** `attaches?` validates and analyses\n  every carried vector (and every install image) by attaching it into a\n  throwaway receiver, and restore attaches it again. Codex found it pure\n  and only duplicated CPU. Accept, or ask the kernel for a validate-only\n  entry point?\n- **Q9, Law 2 outside a fresh receiver.** The id counter is adopted as\n  `max(local, carried)`; a receiver whose construction mints more ids\n  than the carrier relifts to a different root (draft §9 already scopes\n  the law to the fresh-receiver context). No harness case has a\n  non-fresh receiver. Add one as a documented non-law, or leave it?\n- **Q10, the `:running` wire phase's future.** If Q1 narrows the phase\n  set, row 11 disappears from the matrix and the `:install` row's phase\n  slot becomes a constant; if not, row 11 stays a synthetic case. Which?\n\n---\n\n## 12. Portability notes for the implementer\n\n1. **Nil-valued window keys (Dart, Node).** The window is built with\n   `select-keys` and encoded as `[r [:lit nil]]`; never filter `some?`\n   values; `frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member` over row 22\n   (`(pair nil (next-of c))`, the nil operand evaluated before the read)\n   is the canary. Membership is always by `contains?`/key sets, never\n   `(get W r)`.\n2. **`for` over large seqs (Dart).** Row sets of the C4 modules run to\n   thousands of rows; build with `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`.\n3. **Reader whitespace before closers (Dart).** Pinned row sets are\n   Clojure data in `.cljc`, never EDN strings.\n4. **Duplicate protocol params (Dart).** `gc-children [_ _x]`\n   (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:386`); any new protocol method\n   in `layout` likewise.\n5. **Multi-key `assoc` on nil (Dart).** Frames and windows start from\n   `{}` (`handoff.cljc:1455-1457` records the trap).\n6. **Reader conditionals.** `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`\n   with `:cljd` first; `:clj`-only source-reading tests as `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`.\n7. **Private var access (Dart).** Every function named in §8 is public;\n   no `#'`.\n8. **Sorted keys.** Window and bindings rows are sorted by Jing\n   canonical bytes of the key, never by host `sort` on mixed types.\n9. **Numeric classes (Node).** `pc`, `rd`, `r`, `site` are exact\n   integers; compare with `cbor/numeric-kind` before `=` on wire values\n   (`ucf/profile-engine`, `ucf.cljc:77-91`, is the template).\n10. **Fresh worktree.** `mise trust` and `npm ci` before the Node lane.","numLines":143,"startLine":2090,"totalLines":2233}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PMFeT9T124kGivU5vtZEnD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
